compared Instinct, Grok Bot, ChatGPT, and Hermes to see what they can access and how they handle your data. Plus, how to use AI to secure your Google…\n\nSep 2 • Peter Yang\n\n32\n\n2\n\n3\n\n### August 2026\n\nHow Non-Coders Are Vibe Coding $100K+ Businesses With AI | Amol Jain\n\n3 examples of vibe-coded apps that reached six figures in revenue, plus 4 steps to turn your prototype into a real business, including publishing…\n\nAug 30 • Peter Yang\n\n44\n\n2\n\nChatGPT vs. Claude vs. Grok vs. Gemini: The Best AI Across 10 Real Use Cases\n\nMy picks for the best AI models and tools across 10 everyday tasks, plus a preview of my upcoming course for paid subscribers\n\nAug 26 • Peter Yang\n\n46\n\n1\n\nHow to Build Better AI Evals with Claude Code in 5 Steps | Shreya & Hamel\n\nWatch my favorite eval experts do a live audit of the evals that I built for my skills and I promise you’ll learn something new. Plus, their new free…\n\nAug 23 • Peter Yang\n\n45\n\nMy Mom Survived Breast Cancer Three Times. Now We're Fighting It Again With AI's Help\n\nMy mom’s story and how I’m using AI to navigate the healthcare system\n\nAug 19 • Peter Yang\n\n105\n\n17\n\n5--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n14 AI Bots the Grok Bot Team Uses: 8 Practical Tips | CodeMyPixel | CodeMyPixel (https://codemypixel.com/nl/blog/grok-bot-14-tips)\nciteturn24search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: yesterday; Crawled: today; AI·September 27, 2026·4 min leestijd·Door JH Akash ... Peter Yang recently sat down with Peng Zheng and Lauren Tan, the duo behind xAI's Grok Bot project, to walk through the 14 AI bots they actually use every day.\n\nAI·September 27, 2026·4 min leestijd·Door JH Akash\n\n# We Built Grok Bot: 14 AI Bots and 8 Tips Worth Stealing\n\nThe xAI Grok Bot team runs 14 AI bots for design, engineering, and daily life. Here are the 8 practical tips from Peter Yang's interview with Peng Zheng and Lauren Tan.\n\nPeter Yang recently sat down with Peng Zheng and Lauren Tan, the duo behind xAI's Grok Bot project, to walk through the 14 AI bots they actually use every day. Not demos. Not prototypes. Bots that buy supplies, sell things on Facebook Marketplace, book multi-city trips, and review each other's code.\n\nThe full 45-minute conversation is worth your time:\n\nBut if you want the practical lessons, here are the 8 tips worth stealing.\n\n## The big picture: 3 levels of working with AI\n\nPeng describes three tiers, and most people are stuck at level 2:\n\n  1. Do it yourself. You draw the boxes, type the text, send the messages.\n  2. Prompt AI. You ask AI to do each task for you, one by one.\n  3. Design the system. Bots work together with guardrails, on their own.\n\nThe Grok Bot team lives at level 3. Everything below follows from that.\n\n## Tip 1: Get a Chief of Staff bot\n\nPeng's most-used bot is his chief of staff. His rule is simple: \"If I don't know which bot should take the task, it goes to chief of staff.\"\n\nIt bought 3D-printer filament and updated the Notion inventory list by itself. It pays friends back, pulls action items from the inbox, and manages the calendar. One bot for your whole digital life, routing to specialists when needed.\n\nSteal this: instead of ten disconnected chatbots, build one router bot that knows your tools and delegates.\n\n## Tip 2: Put 5 jobs in ONE prompt\n\nPeng sold his DJI mic on Facebook Marketplace with a single prompt. The chief of staff bot:\n\n  1. Checked the official price\n  2. Scanned local competitors\n  3. Asked the writer bot to draft the listing in Peng's own style\n  4. Posted the listing\n  5. Set a rule to drop the price $5 every week if nobody bites\n\nSteal this: stop delegating single tasks. Delegate the whole chain, including the follow-up.\n\n## Tip 3: Let bots debate in a group chat\n\nThe team puts their PM, design, and engineering bots in one group chat and throws in an idea. Each bot argues from its own perspective, with its own memory and preferences. You @mention a bot when you want only that one to reply.\n\nBots even message each other mid-task. During the Marketplace listing, the chief of staff bot consulted the writer bot without being told to.\n\nSteal this: persistent bots with distinct roles beat one giant do-everything prompt.\n\n## Tip 4: You do 5%. The bot does 80%.\n\nPeng draws one key frame. The designer bot scales it into the full user flow, editing Figma directly through the Figma connector and applying his design-system skill. A writer bot polishes every email and Slack message before it goes out, which matters a lot for a non-native English speaker.\n\n\"I do the first 5% and ask it to scale it into the whole flow,\" Peng says.\n\nSteal this: do the part that needs your taste. Delegate the mechanical 80%.\n\n## Tip 5: Climb the Trust Ladder\n\nLauren's assistant bot booked her entire multi-city trip: flights plus hotels through Navan, presenting choices and asking for approval before paying. It has its own phone number, texts her lunch reminders, and orders DoorDash when she's in the zone.\n\nMeanwhile a social bot scans X mentions every 30 minutes and aggregates repeated bug reports into the engineering workflow. Customer feedback becomes the outer loop feeding the inner product loop.\n\nSteal this: approval gates for money and high-risk actions, full autonomy for everything else.\n\n## Tip 8: Build a team that delegates\n\nThe engineering lead bot never does work itself. It breaks projects down, spawns cloud agents, and supervises. Bots verify their own work, one even controls a Linux VM just to test changes, and eval playbooks check whether a change actually improves things before merging. Some PRs auto-merge, and Lauren reviews them after they land.\n\nLauren's goal: \"Not a factory. A Michelin kitchen, quality at scale.\"\n\n## The mindset: treat bots like new hires\n\nPeng's advice for getting started: try everything you touch with keyboard and mouse. Delegate it and see. Start simple, then deliberately overstretch the bot to find its limits. It takes time, like onboarding an employee, and then trust compounds.\n\nAs Peter Yang puts it: \"Now we can all be the CEO.\"\n\n## What this means for your business\n\nYou don't need 14 bots on day one. You need one chief-of-staff-style agent handling your most repetitive workflow, a trust ladder for letting it act, and approval gates where money moves.\n\nThat's exactly what we build at CodeMyPixel: AI agents that automate the repeated work, so your team does the work that matters. Talk to us and we'll map your first bot together.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStart with My Product and Creator Tracks - by Peter Yang (https://creatoreconomy.so/p/product-and-creator-tracks)\nciteturn24search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Crawled: last week; Peter Yang ... First, make sure you’re subscribed to join 55,000+ who get new posts every Wednesday:\n\n# Start with My Product and Creator Tracks\n\n### Level up your PM and creator skills in just 5 min a week\n\nPeter Yang\n\nJul 26, 2023\n\n36\n\n2\n\n1\n\nShare\n\nHello! My goal with this newsletter is to help you:\n\n> Level up your product and creator skills in just 5 min a week.\n\nI’ve led product teams at Amazon, Meta, and Roblox and create content for 200,000+ followers. But I save my best writing for this newsletter.\n\n## Where to start\n\nFirst, make sure you’re subscribed to join 55,000+ who get new posts every Wednesday:\n\nNext, visit the tracks below for my best guides and interviews to help you:\n\n  1. Product track: Level up your product skills and accelerate your PM career.\n\n  2. Creator track: Build a 100K+ follower creator business while working 9-5.\n\nMore info about each track below.\n\n## Product track\n\nLevel up your product skills and accelerate your PM career.\n\nVisit the Product Track\n\nThis track has my best guides and interviews after 10+ years working at 