d actionable stuff, like events I need to RSVP to.\nUnfortunately, Loving Husband is still just a @bot, not a robot, so it can’t actually go to the store and return my wife's items for me, but maybe someday!\nSee me walk through all 11 bots here: https://youtu.be/l6cr7gy5l9E\nPeter Yang: The 11 Grok Bots I still use today are very different from the ones I set up at launch.\nHere's my new video that shows you how to set up:\n→ 3 bots to orchestrate tasks, plan long-term, and maintain my other bots\n→ 3 work bots to monitor YouTube, X, and business metrics\n→ 5\n\nSep 14, 06:00 PM ET View post\n\nImage: petergyang\n\npetergyang @petergyang\n\nOne of my Grok bots is called Loving Husband.\nIt helps me manage school emails and requests from my spouse.\nFor example, every Sunday, it reads my kids’ school’s long email and gives me only the takeaways and actionable stuff, like events I need to RSVP to.\nUnfortunately, Loving Husband is still just a @bot, not a robot, so it can’t actually go to the store and return my wife's items for me, but maybe someday!\nSee me walk through all 11 bots here: https://youtu.be/l6cr7gy5l9E\n\n* * *\n\nPeter Yang: The 11 Grok Bots I still use today are very different from the ones I set up at launch.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow Creators Can Get Started with AI - by Peter Yang (https://creatoreconomy.so/p/how-creators-can-get-started-with)\nciteturn24search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.0 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; Peter Yang ... If you’re a creator, consider giving AI tools a try to learn how they can help you create better content. ... For example, to convert text to images, a ML model needs to learn from millions of images with text labels. ... How the model works is a black box even to ML engineers, so the user experience needs to be clear, believable, and actionable.\n\n# How Creators Can Get Started with AI\n\n### How does AI work anyway and how you can start playing with AI tools now\n\nPeter Yang\n\nOct 05, 2022\n\nDear subscribers,\n\n> I believe that AI will dramatically lower the cost of creation in the next few years.\n\nIf you’re a creator, consider giving AI tools a try to learn how they can help you create better content. To guide you through this, I’ll expand on my AI art post to explore:\n\n  1. How does AI work anyway?\n\n  2. How is AI lowering the cost of creation?\n\n  3. How can creators get started with AI?\n\n* * *\n\n## How does AI work anyway?\n\nI think every creator should have a basic understanding of how AI works. I’m far from an expert, but let’s start with a few definitions:\n\n  1. AI is when machines demonstrate intelligence at the human level and beyond.\n\n  2. Machine learning (ML) is how machines learn to be intelligent by identifying patterns in data. ML models have two artifacts:\n\n    1. Label: The output that the machine wants to predict (e.g., video watch time).\n\n    2. Features: Inputs to achieve the output (e.g., video views and likes).\n\nAt a high level, machine learning works in 3 steps:\n\nPrepare data, train model, and build UX\n\n  1. Prepare data: Machines need lots of quality data to learn. For example, to convert text to images, a ML model needs to learn from millions of images with text labels. ML engineers typically spend 80% of their time manually cleaning the data in a process called feature engineering.\n\n  2. Train model: Next, ML engineers split the data into a training set and a test set. The machine uses the training set to build the model and then uses the test set to improve the model’s accuracy. The model’s algorithms can be:\n\n    1. Simple like a linear regression (e.g., a person’s weight = 80 + 2 * height). The model will adjust the feature weights (e.g., change 2 to 1.5) through repeat iterations to more accurately predict the label (output).\n\n    2. Complex like a neural network. The model will not only assign weights to features but also create new features automatically. Most models based on images or natural language use neural networks.\n\n  3. Build user experience: After training the model, the team needs to build a UX where people can supply inputs to get their desired output. How the model works is a black box even to ML engineers, so the user experience needs to be clear, believable, and actionable.\n\nOk, that’s a mouthful. If you remember one thing, it’s this:\n\n> Machines need large quality data sets to learn. They get better by identifying patterns and predicting outcomes through repeat iterations.\n\nIf you want to learn more, I highly recommend Google’s ML crash course. You don’t need to know how to code to understand the basics.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n7 Days of AI: Advance from AI Beginner to Proficient in Just 7 Days (https://ai.creatoreconomy.so/)\nciteturn24search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.3 years ago; # 7 days of AI by Peter Yang ... # 7 days of AI by Peter Yang\n# 7 days of AI by Peter Yang\n\n## Advance from AI Beginner to Proficient in Just 7 Days\n\nEverything you need to use AI to create better content, save time, and improve your daily life.\n\n[Input: Enter your email]\n\n[Input: Telephone]\n\n[Button: Send Me the Guide + Lesson 1]\n\n[Input]\n\nTrusted by 70,000+ readers from:\n\nImage\n\nImage: Learn How to Create with AI in Just 7 Days\n\n### Here's what you'll get for free\n\n7 guides, 20+ prompts, and 4,000+ words to help you use AI to achieve your goals today Day 1. How Large Language Models (LLMs) Actually Work Day 2. 3 Essential Tips to Level Up Your AI Prompts Day 3. How to Build Your Personal AI Writing Assistant Day 4. How to Create GPTs to Do Your Work for You Day 5. My Favorite AI Tools to Create Images, Videos, and More Day 6. 5 Ways to Use AI to Improve Your Everyday Life Day 7. How Product Teams at Top Companies Build AI Products\n\n  * Substack\n  * Twitter\n  * LinkedIn\n\n# 7 days of AI by Peter Yang\n\n# Welcome to 7 days of AI\n\nHi there,Congrats on taking your first step to learning how to create with AI.I’ve spent 100s of hours using the best AI tools that are available today. I’ve also built AI products and interviewed top leaders in the field.My goal is to give you everything that you need to start using AI to create amazing things, save time, and improve your daily life.\n\n## Here’s what we’ll cover in 7 days:\n\nDay 1. How Large Language Models (LLMs) Actually Work Day 2. 3 Essential Tips to Level Up Your AI Prompts Day 3. How to Build Your Personal AI Writing Assistant Day 4. How to Create GPTs to Do Your Work for You Day 5. My Favorite AI Tools to Create Images, Videos, and More Day 6. 5 Ways to Use AI to Improve Your Everyday Life Day 7. How Product Teams at Top Companies Build AI Products\n\nNow go check your inbox and let's dive into day 1.Best,\nPeter--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHands On: Grok Bot | The Aigentic (https://www.theaigentic.com/hands-on/grok-bot)\nciteturn24search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: yesterday; A working setup guide for xAI's agent teammates — the bots worth building first, the routines that actually stick, and the permission rules to set before you hand over your inbox.September 3, 2026 · The Aigentic ... Grok Bot went into early beta on August 11, 2026, and xAI described it in one line that is easy to skim past: \"your team of always-on agents.\" ... Four creators have now put real hours into it and published what they run: Claire Vo, who moved almost her entire agent stack over; Peter Yang, who compared it head-to-head against local and cloud rivals; Alex Finn, who runs it as a business org chart; and Matthew Berman, who wired it into his daily inbox and calendar.\n\n# Hands On: Grok Bot\n\nA working setup guide for xAI's agent teammates — the bots worth building first, the routines that actually stick, and the permission rules to set before you hand over your inbox.\n\nSeptember 3, 2026 · The Aigentic\n\n  * Grok Bot\n  * agents\n  * xAI\n\nImage: Hands On: Grok Bot\n\nGrok Bot went into early beta on August 11, 2026, and xAI described it in one line that is easy to skim past: \"your team of always-on agents.\" The important word is team. This is not a chat window with a better memory. Each Bot gets its own cloud computer, its own browser, its own logins, and its own standing instructions, and it keeps working when your laptop is shut.\n\nThat changes what you should build. A chat assistant rewards good prompts. A team of agents rewards good org design — narrow roles, explicit boundaries, and scheduled work that runs whether or not you show up.\n\nFour creators have now put real hours into it and published what they run: Claire Vo, who moved almost her entire agent stack over; Peter Yang, who compared it head-to-head against local and cloud rivals; Alex Finn, who runs it as a business org chart; and Matthew Berman, who wired it into his daily inbox and calendar. This guide pulls their setups into one path you can follow today, in order, with the guardrails they learned the hard way.\n\nA note on honesty before we start: this is beta software, and much of what follows comes from creator demonstrations rather than documentation. Where something is unverified, we say so.\n\n## Before you start: what you actually need\n\nxAI lists Grok Bot as available on desktop and iOS for SuperGrok, SuperGrok Plus, and SuperGrok Heavy, plus Cursor Pro, Pro+, and Ultra, and Cursor Teams Standard and Premium. Enterprise access is a waitlist.\n\nOne detail from the announcement is worth internalizing because it changes how freely you delegate: Grok Bot usage is separate from your Grok or Cursor plan, and work you hand to a Bot does not count against your existing usage. xAI does not publish dollar prices or numeric Bot limits on that page, so ignore any figure you see quoted secondhand, including in the videos below.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPeter Yang (@petergyang): \"I am a big fan of Grok @bot, and it’s clear that other personal agents are moving toward using a persistent cloud computer. But there’s just something that feels wrong about entering my password and authentication code into a cloud browser. Beyond supporting more official plugins, I’m not sure there are easy solutions to this. I talk more about it in my latest video here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Yeo6M8-W_8\" | nitter (https://x.noodl3.net/petergyang/status/2095217706647851168)\nciteturn24search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Peter Yang ... I am a big fan of Grok @bot, and it’s clear that other personal agents are moving toward using a persistent cloud computer. ... Sep 2, 2026 · 6:30 PM UTC\nPeter Yang\n\n@petergyang\n\nSep 2\n\nI am a big fan of Grok @bot, and it’s clear that other personal agents are moving toward using a persistent cloud computer. But there’s just something that feels wrong about entering my password and authentication code into a cloud browser. Beyond supporting more official plugins, I’m not sure there are easy solutions to this. I talk more about it in my latest video here: piped.video/watch?v=6Yeo6M8-…\n\nALT Screenshot of an Amazon two-step verification page open inside a cloud browser.\n\nImage Peter Yang\n\n@petergyang\n\nSep 2\n\nWhich AI personal agent can you trust with your data? In my new tutorial, I compared Instinct, Grok Bot, ChatGPT, and Hermes to answer: 1. What do their privacy policies actually say about how they're handling your data? 2. How safe is it to log in and complete 2FA on a cloud computer? 3. What’s the actual risk here, based on a real example? I also show how to use AI to audit and remove 3rd party apps connected to your Google Account. I had more than 80. 🥲 📌 Watch the full video now: piped.video/6Yeo6M8-W_8\n\nSep 2, 2026 · 6:30 PM UTC\n\n80\n\n16\n\n365\n\n106,003\n\nMichael Truell\